Subject: [PATCH DRAFT] regulator: proxy-consumer: drop load before enable

Remove the proxy consumer's current-load vote before dropping its enable
reference. ACK regulator core can run a DRMS update after decrementing
the final consumer enable_count; if that synchronous update fails, the
disable call can return an error after the reference has already been
decremented. Clearing the load first moves that failure-prone transition
ahead of the decrement on the normal path.

Keep best-effort cleanup behavior if load-zero itself fails; this hook
runs from regulator_unregister(), which cannot be vetoed by this void
cleanup path. This patch does not claim to solve a persistent provider
disable failure or the managed-handle warning that can follow it.

This is an isolated AI1 candidate against the AI2 ROUND31/32/33 source
series. It does not modify the AI2 branch.

---
 drivers/regulator/proxy-consumer.c | 34 +++++++++++++++++++---------------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)
diff --git a/drivers/regulator/proxy-consumer.c b/drivers/regulator/proxy-consumer.c
--- a/drivers/regulator/proxy-consumer.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/proxy-consumer.c
@@ -375,25 +375,29 @@
 		list_del(&consumer->list);
 		kfree(consumer);
 		return 0;
 	}
 
-	if (consumer->enable) {
-		ret = regulator_disable(consumer->reg);
-		if (ret) {
-			pr_err("regulator_disable failed, rc=%d\n", ret);
-			rc = ret;
-		}
-	}
-
-	if (consumer->current_uA > 0) {
-		ret = regulator_set_load(consumer->reg, 0);
-		if (ret < 0) {
-			pr_err("regulator_set_load failed, rc=%d\n", ret);
-			rc = ret;
-		}
-	}
+	/*
+	 * Clear the load vote before dropping the enable reference. The
+	 * regulator core may run DRMS after decrementing the last reference.
+	 */
+	if (consumer->current_uA > 0) {
+		ret = regulator_set_load(consumer->reg, 0);
+		if (ret < 0) {
+			pr_err("regulator_set_load failed, rc=%d\n", ret);
+			rc = ret;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (consumer->enable) {
+		ret = regulator_disable(consumer->reg);
+		if (ret) {
+			pr_err("regulator_disable failed, rc=%d\n", ret);
+			rc = ret;
+		}
+	}
 
 	if (consumer->voltage_set) {
 		ret = regulator_set_voltage(consumer->reg, 0, INT_MAX);
 		if (ret) {
 			pr_err("regulator_set_voltage failed, rc=%d\n", ret);
